<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  modules/commons/pom.xml — Stage 2-b "the reactor's first real dependency"
  module POM (task-1-brief.md).

  STATUS: ADDITIVE and RUN BY HAND (mvn -pl modules/commons -am package from
  the reactor root, or mvn -f modules/commons/pom.xml package). Ant
  (modules/commons/build.xml + build.incl.xml) remains the real build;
  nothing here is wired into CMake or any release build yet.

  WHY THIS MODULE. Stage 2-a (task-2-brief.md) chose modules/localization
  specifically BECAUSE it is a leaf (zero imports) and skipped commons for
  the opposite reason: commons imports org.scilab.modules.localization.*.
  That is exactly why Stage 2-b picks it up next — it is the cheapest module
  that exercises a REAL reactor dependency edge, proving Maven resolves
  build order from the dependency graph rather than from prebuildjava/
  build.xml:25's hand-encoded 23-module <target name="all" depends="...">
  list (localization, commons, history_manager, jvm, ... in a fixed, manually
  maintained sequence). See Step 5 below and task-1-report.md for the actual
  reactor-ordering proof.

  STEP 1 FINDINGS (grep -rhE '^import (org|com)\.' modules/commons/src/java,
  25 files, confirmed against task-1-brief.md's expectation, then verified
  FURTHER — a plain import grep is not the whole story):
    - org.scilab.modules.commons.*      — itself (8 in-package imports)
    - org.scilab.modules.localization.* — Messages, WindowsDefaultLanguage:
      the reactor dependency, below.
    - org.flexdock.view.View            — thirdparty/flexdock-1.2.5.jar,
      real load-bearing usage (ScilabGUIUtilities.toFront(View, String)),
      not a stray unused import. See the flexdock dependency below.
    - org.w3c.dom.*, org.xml.sax.*      — JDK (java.xml module), not a
      dependency at all.
  No `manifest.class-path` in build.xml (confirmed again in the actual
  Ant-built jar's manifest: the Class-Path attribute is the literal,
  never-interpolated string "${manifest.class-path}", same as localization).

  A WIDER SCAN THAN THE BRIEF ASKED FOR, BECAUSE FOUR MORE FULLY-QUALIFIED
  NAMES SHOWED UP under a plain `grep -o '(org|com)\.[a-zA-Z0-9_.]+'` sweep
  (i.e. not restricted to `^import` lines) and each needed to be individually
  ruled in or out as a real compile-time dependency rather than assumed away:
    - org.scilab.modules.core.Scilab — ScilabCommonsUtils.java:207, inside a
      JAVADOC COMMENT ("Called from org.scilab.modules.core.Scilab::..."),
      not code. Zero compile-time weight.
    - org.scilab.modules.history_manager.HistoryPrefs — XConfiguration.
      java:108, org.scilab.modules.jvm.LoadClassPath — ScilabCommonsUtils.
      java:167,184, org.scilab.modules.preferences.ScilabPreferences —
      ScilabGeneralPrefs.java:45: all THREE reached only via
      ClassLoader.getSystemClassLoader().loadClass("...") /
      Class.forName("...") — STRING literals passed to reflection, never an
      `import` or a static type reference. javac never needs to resolve
      these at compile time, so none of history_manager/jvm/preferences
      belongs in <dependencies> below. This is very likely deliberate: those
      three modules plausibly depend back on commons, so a compile-time
      import here would cycle; reflection is how the Ant-built tree already
      avoids that, and the Maven POM must reproduce the SAME absence, not
      "fix" it into a real dependency edge.
    - com.sun.nio.file.SensitivityWatchEventModifier (FileSystemMonitor.
      java:139) and the four com.sun.org.apache.{xalan,xerces,xpath}.
      internal.* / com.sun.xml.internal.stream.XMLOutputFactoryImpl JAXP
      factory-override class NAMES (ScilabXPathFactory.java:27,
      ScilabDocumentBuilderFactory.java:27, ScilabXMLOutputFactory.java:25,
      ScilabTransformerFactory.java:27): all four are `private static final
      String` constants (JDK-internal implementation class names for the
      JAXP factory-override system-property mechanism) or a Class.forName
      argument — never imported, never used as a type. They live inside the
      JDK's own java.xml module already (internal, non-exported packages)
      and resolve at RUNTIME via reflection, not at compile time. Not a
      dependency; nothing to add.
  Net result: Step 1's dependency inventory is exactly two entries —
  localization (reactor) and flexdock (vendored thirdparty) — matching the
  brief's expectation, but confirmed by ruling out every other fully-
  qualified name in the source rather than trusting the import-only grep.
-->
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

  <parent>
    <groupId>org.scilab</groupId>
    <artifactId>scilab-modules-parent</artifactId>
    <version>2027.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    <relativePath>../../pom.xml</relativePath>
  </parent>

  <artifactId>commons</artifactId>
  <packaging>jar</packaging>

  <!-- build.xml:19 <property name="library.title" value="Commons for Scilab modules"/> -->
  <name>Commons for Scilab modules</name>

  <dependencies>
    <!-- THE REACTOR DEPENDENCY — the mechanic this whole task exists to
         prove. Replaces the hand-encoded ordering at prebuildjava/
         build.xml:25 (`depends="scirenderer, localization, commons, ..."`)
         for this one edge: Maven computes that `commons` needs
         `localization` built first from THIS <dependency> declaration
         alone, not from <modules> declaration order in the parent POM
         (commons is added to <modules> below in scilab/pom.xml AFTER
         localization only for human readability — Step 5 in
         task-1-report.md proves `mvn -pl modules/commons -am package`
         builds localization first even with that ordering ignored, by
         rerunning it with <modules> order left as-is and watching the
         reactor still sequence localization ahead of commons on the
         dependency graph). version uses ${project.version} (not a literal
         2027.0.0-SNAPSHOT) so it can never drift from the parent's actual
         version — the ordinary Maven reactor-sibling idiom. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.scilab</groupId>
      <artifactId>localization</artifactId>
      <version>${project.version}</version>
    </dependency>
    <!-- FLEXDOCK — a PERMANENTLY VENDORED third-party jar, not scaffolding
         awaiting a coordinate. modules/prebuildjava/ivy.xml:29 carries a
         commented-out flexdock <dependency> element tagged COPY (ivy's own
         convention, used on this line and its neighbors, for "give up
         resolving this and hand-copy it instead") — ivy's own author gave
         up resolving it from any repository and left it to be hand-copied
         from thirdparty/flexdock-1.2.5.jar instead. It is not
         on Maven Central (or any repository this build reaches) under any
         coordinate, and — per task-1-brief.md — that will still be true
         after this task, unlike Stage 2-a's beachhead where every "not
         wired into CMake yet" mechanism was explicitly temporary. Ant
         itself does not resolve this jar either: build.incl.xml:80's
         compile.classpath just points a raw <pathelement> at
         ${flexdock.jar} = thirdparty/flexdock-1.2.5.jar
         (scilab-lib.properties:11), i.e. a fixed local file path with no
         dependency-resolution machinery at all.

         MECHANISM CHOSEN: <scope>system</scope> + <systemPath>, the direct
         Maven analogue of Ant's raw pathelement — no repository, no
         resolution, just "put this exact file on the compile classpath."
         Two alternatives considered and rejected:
           - install:install-file into ~/.m2 under a squatted
             org.flexdock:flexdock:1.2.5 coordinate: this is an imperative,
             out-of-band, PER-MACHINE step that leaves no trace in version
             control. A fresh clone + `mvn package` would fail until
             someone manually re-runs the install command (or it is bound
             as a plugin execution — which then reduces to installing into
             the shared ~/.m2 namespace on every build, a bigger footprint
             for no behavioral gain over systemPath here, since this jar is
             consumed the same way Ant already consumes it: a raw file, not
             a published artifact).
           - a project-local file:// repository laid out in Maven's
             groupId/artifactId/version directory shape: reproducible from
             a fresh clone, but requires DUPLICATING or symlinking the jar
             into a repo-shaped subtree under version control (thirdparty/
             is flat and populated by fetch-thirdparty.sh, not laid out as
             a Maven repo) — more moving parts than systemPath for the same
             outcome, and a second on-disk copy of the same permanently-
             vendored jar to keep in sync.
         Trade-off accepted with systemPath: `system` scope is excluded
         from transitive resolution (a consumer of commons would not
         inherit flexdock automatically) and is generally discouraged for
         PUBLISHED library dependencies — irrelevant here, since this
         reactor is additive/run-by-hand and not published to any
         repository; every module that needs flexdock (gui, scinotes, ...,
         per build.incl.xml's compile.classpath) will declare the same
         systemPath dependency itself when its turn in the migration comes,
         exactly as each of their build.xml files independently references
         ${flexdock.jar} today. This IS the production mechanism for this
         jar going forward, not a placeholder. -->
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.flexdock</groupId>
      <artifactId>flexdock</artifactId>
      <version>1.2.5</version>
      <scope>system</scope>
      <systemPath>${project.basedir}/../../thirdparty/flexdock-1.2.5.jar</systemPath>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>

  <build>
    <!-- build.incl.xml:20 <property name="src.dir" location="src/java/"/>.
         Same source tree Ant compiles — see modules/localization/pom.xml's
         identical comment; nothing module-specific to add here. -->
    <sourceDirectory>src/java</sourceDirectory>

    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-jar-plugin</artifactId>
        <configuration>
          <archive>
            <!-- addMavenDescriptor=false and the Built-By entry are
                 inherited from the parent POM — not redeclared here. Same
                 <manifestFile> mechanism as modules/localization/pom.xml
                 (see that file's <build> comment for why manifestSections/
                 manifestEntries, the mechanism task-1-brief.md Step 4
                 pointed at by cross-reference to Step 4 of task-2-brief.md,
                 does not work: wrong attribute order and a
                 "${manifest.class-path}" -> literal "null" corruption).
                 MANIFEST-section.MF's bytes were EXTRACTED from the actual
                 Ant-built jar's META-INF/MANIFEST.MF
                 (unzip -p modules/commons/jar/org.scilab.modules.commons.jar
                 META-INF/MANIFEST.MF), never retyped: Specification-Vendor
                 and Implementation-Vendor both carry the double-mangled
                 "Ã¨" (bytes c3 83 c2 a8) that Ant's <property file> produces
                 by decoding scilab.properties's UTF-8 "è" (c3 a8) as
                 Latin-1 and re-encoding the result as UTF-8 — see
                 modules/localization/src/main/manifest/MANIFEST-section.MF
                 and task-2-report.md section 2 for the mechanism. Retyping
                 a linguistically-correct "è" here would compile, look
                 right to a human, and fail parity silently. Implementation-
                 Version (the DSTAMP/TSTAMP live timestamp) is left out for
                 the same reason localization's is: parity/fingerprint.py's
                 _MANIFEST_VOLATILE strips it from both sides before
                 comparing, so present-and-stripped and absent-and-never-
                 added are byte-identical after normalization. -->
            <manifestFile>${project.basedir}/src/main/manifest/MANIFEST-section.MF</manifestFile>
          </archive>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </build>
</project>
